  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "$$\n\n$\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometry identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
